-- Populate the "OSI Model Data Encapsulation & PDU Flow" article
UPDATE articles
SET content = E'## OSI Model Data Encapsulation & Protocol Data Unit (PDU) Flow\n\nThe Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model is a seven-layer conceptual framework standardized by ISO/IEC 7498-1 that describes how data moves from an application on one device to an application on another. Each layer adds its own header (and sometimes trailer) as data descends the stack before transmission, a process called encapsulation. The receiving device reverses the process through de-encapsulation.\n\nUnderstanding OSI encapsulation is fundamental to network troubleshooting because it tells you exactly which layer and which device type is responsible when communication fails.\n\n## The Seven Layers & Their PDUs\n\n| Layer | Name | PDU Name | Function | Key Protocols | Devices |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 7 | Application | Data | User-facing services and APIs | HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS, HL7 | Gateways, Load Balancers |\n| 6 | Presentation | Data | Encryption, compression, format translation | TLS/SSL, JPEG, ASCII, EBCDIC | - |\n| 5 | Session | Data | Session establishment, maintenance, teardown | NetBIOS, RPC, SMB | - |\n| 4 | Transport | Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) | End-to-end delivery, flow control, error recovery | TCP, UDP | Firewalls (stateful) |\n| 3 | Network | Packet | Logical addressing and routing between networks | IP, ICMP, OSPF, BGP | Routers, L3 Switches |\n| 2 | Data Link | Frame | Physical addressing (MAC), error detection | Ethernet 802.3, Wi-Fi 802.11, ARP | Switches, Bridges |\n| 1 | Physical | Bits | Electrical/optical/radio signal transmission | Cat5e/6, Fiber, 802.11 radio | Hubs, NICs, Cables, APs |\n\n## The Encapsulation Process (Sending)\n\n**Step 1 - Application/Presentation/Session (Layers 7-5):**\nThe application generates data. For a healthcare example: an EHR client sends an HL7 ADT (Admit/Discharge/Transfer) message to the hospital information system. TLS encrypts the payload at Layer 6. A session ID tracks the transaction at Layer 5.\n\n**Step 2 - Transport Layer (Layer 4):**\nTCP wraps the data with a header containing source port, destination port, sequence number, acknowledgement number, and checksum. The PDU is now called a Segment. For HL7 over TCP, the default port is 2575.\n\n**Step 3 - Network Layer (Layer 3):**\nIP adds a header with the source IP address (sending workstation) and destination IP address (HL7 interface engine). The PDU is now called a Packet. The router uses this header to make forwarding decisions.\n\n**Step 4 - Data Link Layer (Layer 2):**\nAn Ethernet header is prepended with source MAC and destination MAC addresses. A Frame Check Sequence (FCS) trailer is appended for error detection via CRC. The PDU is now a Frame. Switches use the MAC addresses to forward within the local network.\n\n**Step 5 - Physical Layer (Layer 1):**\nThe frame is serialized into a bitstream of electrical voltages (copper), light pulses (fiber), or radio waves (Wi-Fi). The PDU is Bits.\n\n## De-Encapsulation Process (Receiving)\n\nThe receiving device strips headers in reverse order:\n1. NIC reads bits from the wire and reconstructs the Frame\n2. Data Link layer verifies FCS checksum, strips Ethernet header/trailer\n3. Network layer reads destination IP, confirms it matches, strips IP header\n4. Transport layer reassembles segments in sequence order, strips TCP header\n5. Application layer delivers the decrypted, decompressed data to the HL7 listener\n\n## Healthcare IT Context\n\nIn a hospital network, a single HL7 ADT message traverses all seven layers:\n- **Layer 7:** Epic sends an ADT^A01 message via the HL7 protocol\n- **Layer 4:** TCP ensures reliable delivery (retransmits lost segments)\n- **Layer 3:** The packet routes from the clinical VLAN (10.10.10.x) to the integration engine VLAN (10.10.20.x)\n- **Layer 2:** The frame switches between Access and Distribution layer switches\n- **Layer 1:** Signals traverse Cat6A cabling to the server room patch panel\n\nIf the message fails, OSI layering helps isolate the problem: Can you ping the destination (Layer 3)? Is the port open (Layer 4)? Is the HL7 parser rejecting the message format (Layer 7)?\n\n## Mnemonic Aids\n\n**Top-down (Layer 7 to 1):** All People Seem To Need Data Processing\n**Bottom-up (Layer 1 to 7):** Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away\n\n## CompTIA A+ Core 1 Exam Relevance\n\nMaps to CompTIA A+ Core 1 (220-1201) Objectives:\n- **2.1:** Compare and contrast networking hardware (associate device types with OSI layers)\n- **2.3:** Compare and contrast protocols, ports, and their purposes\n- **5.2:** Given a scenario, troubleshoot common network issues (identify which OSI layer a symptom maps to)\n\n## References & Citations\n\n- [ISO/IEC 7498-1: Open Systems Interconnection Basic Reference Model](https://www.iso.org/standard/20269.html) - The original OSI standard\n- [Cisco Press: Interconnecting Cisco Network Devices](https://www.ciscopress.com/store/interconnecting-cisco-network-devices-part-1-icnd1-9780132877435) - Data encapsulation flowcharts\n- [RFC 791: Internet Protocol (IPv4)](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791) - Layer 3 packet structure\n- [HL7 International: HL7 v2.x Protocol](https://www.hl7.org/implement/standards/) - Healthcare Layer 7 messaging standard',
    is_featured = true
WHERE id = 'c02b3573-1937-4b63-9642-01371f476bbb';